ws              | Linux                      |\n     9→+--------+-------+-----+--------+-------+-----+--------+-----+-------+\n    10→| x86‑64 | arm64 | x86 | x86‑64 | arm64 | x86 | x86‑64 | arm | arm64 |\n    11→+========+=======+=====+========+=======+=====+========+=====+=======+\n    12→|        |       |     |        |       | |v| | |f|    | |v| | |v|   |\n    13→+--------+-------+-----+--------+-------+-----+--------+-----+-------+\n    14→\n    15→`Flatpak <https://flatpak.org>`__ provides a way for developers to distribute\n    16→apps to Linux users in a format that is independent of the specific distribution\n    17→used by the end-user. It allow packaging applications for use on any common\n    18→Linux distribution, including Ubuntu, Debian, Fedora, and more. There are some\n    19→system packages needed to run and build Flatpaks; see the `Flatpak setup guide\n    20→<https://flatpak.org/setup>`__ for more details.\n    21→\n    22→A Flatpak app is built by compiling against a ``runtime``. Runtimes provide the\n    23→basic dependencies that are used by applications. Each application must be built\n    24→against a runtime, and this runtime must be installed on a host system in order\n    25→for the application to run (Flatpak can automatically install the runtime\n    26→required by an application).\n    27→\n    28→The end user will install the Flatpak into their local app repository; this can\n    29→be done by installing directly from a single file ``.flatpak`` bundle, or by\n    30→installing from a package repository like `Flathub <https://flathub.org>`__.\n    31→Apps can be installed into user-space, or if the user has sufficient privileges,\n    32→they can be installed into a system-wide app repository.\n    33→\n    34→Briefcase currently supports creating ``.flatpak`` single file bundles; end users\n    35→can install the app bundle by running:\n    36→\n    37→.. code-block:: console\n    38→\n    39→    $ flatpak install --user App_Name-1.2.3-x86_64.flatpak\n    40→\n    41→substituting the name of the flatpak file as appropriate. The ``--user`` option\n    42→can be omitted if the user wants to install the app system-wide.\n    43→\n    44→The app can then be run with:\n    45→\n    46→.. code-block:: console\n    47→\n    48→    $ flatpak run com.example.appname\n    49→\n    50→specifying the app bundle identifier as appropriate.\n    51→\n    52→Briefcase *can* be published to Flathub or another Flatpak repository; but\n    53→Briefcase does not currently support automated publication of apps.\n    54→\n    55→Icon format\n    56→===========\n    57→\n    58→Flatpak uses ``.png`` format icons. An application must provide icons in\n    59→the following sizes:\n    60→\n    61→* 16px\n    62→* 32px\n    63→* 64px\n    64→* 128px\n    65→* 256px\n    66→* 512px\n    67→\n    68→Flatpaks do not support splash screens or installer images.\n    69→\n    70→Application configuration\n    71→=========================\n    72→\n    73→The following options can be added to the\n    74→``tool.briefcase.app.<appname>.linux.flatpak`` section of your\n    75→``pyproject.toml`` file:\n    76→\n    77→``finish_arg``\n    78→~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n    79→\n    80→The arguments used to configure the Flatpak sandbox. ``finish_arg`` is an attribute\n    81→that can have additional sub-attributes; each sub-attribute maps to a single property\n    82→that will be added to the app's manifest. For example, to add ``--allow=bluetooth`` as a\n    83→finish argument, you would specify::\n    84→\n    85→    finish_arg.\"allow=bluetooth\" = True\n    86→\n    87→Briefcase adds the following finish arguments by default:\n    88→\n    89→* ``share=ipc``\n    90→* ``socket=x11``\n    91→* ``nosocket=wayland``\n    92→* ``share=network``\n    93→* ``device=dri``\n    94→* ``socket=pulseaudio``\n    95→* ``filesystem=xdg-cache``\n    96→* ``filesystem=xdg-config``\n    97→* ``filesystem=xdg-data``\n    98→* ``filesystem=xdg-documents``\n    99→* ``socket=session-bus``\n   100→\n   101→These can be disabled by explicitly setting their value to ``False``; for example, to\n   102→disable audio access, you would specify::\n   103→\n   104→    finish_arg.\"socket=pulseaudio\" = false\n   105→\n   106→``flatpak_runtime_repo_alias``\n   107→~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n   108→\n   109→An alias to use when registering the Flatpak repository that will store the\n   110→Flatpak runtime used to build the app. By default, Briefcase will use `Flathub\n   111→<https://flathub.org>`__ as its runtime repository, with an alias of ``flathub``.\n   112→\n   113→``flatpak_runtime_repo_url``\n   114→~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n   115→\n   116→The repository URL hosting the runtime and SDK package that the Flatpak will\n   117→use. By default, Briefcase will use `Flathub <https://flathub.org>`__ as its\n   118→runtime repository.\n   119→\n   120→``flatpak_runtime``\n   121→~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n   122→\n   123→A string, identifying the `runtime\n   124→<https://docs.flatpak.org/en/latest/available-runtimes.html>`__ to use as a\n   125→base for the Flatpak app.\n   126→\n   127→The Flatpak runtime and SDK are paired; so, both a ``flatpak_runtime`` and a\n   128→corresponding ``flatpak_sdk`` must be defined.\n   129→\n   130→``flatpak_runtime_version``\n   131→~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n   132→\n   133→A string, identifying the version of the Flatpak runtime that should be used.\n   134→\n   135→``flatpak_sdk``\n   136→~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n   137→\n   138→A string, identifying the SDK associated with the platform that will be used to\n   139→build the Flatpak app.\n   140→\n   141→The Flatpak runtime and SDK are paired; so, both a ``flatpak_runtime`` and a\n   142→corresponding ``flatpak_sdk`` must be defined.\n   143→\n   144→``modules_extra_content``\n   145→~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n   146→\n   147→Additional build instructions that will be inserted into the Flatpak manifest, *after*\n   148→Python has been installed and ``pip`` is guaranteed to exist, but *before* any app code\n   149→or app packages have been installed into the Flatpak.\n   150→\n   151→Permissions\n   152→===========\n   153→\n   154→Permissions are not used for Flatpak packaging.\n   155→\n   156→Compilation issues with Flatpak\n   157→===============================\n   158→\n   159→Flatpak works by building a sandbox in which to compile the application bundle.\n   160→This sandbox uses some low-level kernel and file system operations to provide\n   161→the sandboxing behavior. As a result, Flatpaks cannot be built inside a Docker\n   162→container, and they cannot be build on an NFS mounted drive.\n   163→\n   164→If you get errors about ``renameat`` when building an app, similar to the\n   165→following::\n   166→\n   167→    [helloworld] Building Flatpak...\n   168→    Downloading sources\n   169→    Initializing build dir\n   170→    Committing stage init to cache\n   171→    Error: Writing metadata object: renameat: Operation not permitted\n   172→    Building...\n   173→\n   174→    Error while building app helloworld.\n   175→\n   176→    Log saved to ...\n   177→\n   178→you may be building on an NFS drive. Move your project to local storage, and\n   179→retry the build.\n   180→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"010a84c9-f0d8-4259-8033-5fc61c44e166","uuid":"b64a157f-86ae-4cc8-aea1-b8c1a8836813","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/testbed/docs/reference/platforms/linux/flatpak.rst","content":"=======\nFlatpak\n=======\n\n+--------+-------+---------+--------+---+-----+--------+-----+-------+\n| Host Platform Support (:ref:`platform-support-key`)                |\n+--------+-------+---------+--------+---+-----+--------+-----+-------+\n| macOS          | Windows              | Linux                      |\n+--------+-------+-----+--------+-------+-----+--------+-----+-------+\n| x86‑64 | arm64 | x86 | x86‑64 | arm64 | x86 | x86‑64 | arm | arm64 |\n+========+=======+=====+========+=======+=====+========+=====+=======+\n|        |       |     |        |       | |v| | |f|    | |v| | |v|   |\n+--------+-------+-----+--------+-------+-----+--------+-----+-------+\n\n`Flatpak <https://flatpak.org>`__ provides a way for developers to distribute\napps to Linux users in a format that is independent of the specific distribution\nused by the end-user. It allow packaging applications for use on any common\nLinux distribution, including Ubuntu, Debian, Fedora, and more. There are some\nsystem packages needed to run and build Flatpaks; see the `Flatpak setup guide\n<https://flatpak.org/setup>`__ for more details.\n\nA Flatpak app is built by compiling against a ``runtime``. Runtimes provide the\nbasic dependencies that are used by applications. Each application must be built\nagainst a runtime, and this runtime must be installed on a host system in order\nfor the application to run (Flatpak can automatically install the runtime\nrequired by an application).\n\nThe end user will install the Flatpak into their local app repository; this can\nbe done by installing directly from a single file ``.flatpak`` bundle, or by\ninstalling from a package repository like `Flathub <https://flathub.org>`__.\nApps can be installed into user-space, or if the user has sufficient privileges,\nthey can be installed into a system-wide app repository.\n\nBriefcase currently supports creating ``.flatpak`` single file bundles; end users\ncan install the app bundle by running:\n\n.. code-block:: console\n\n    $ flatpak install --user App_Name-1.2.3-x86_64.flatpak\n\nsubstituting the name of the flatpak file as appropriate. The ``--user`` option\ncan be omitted if the user wants to install the app system-wide.\n\nThe app can then be run with:\n\n.. code-block:: console\n\n    $ flatpak run com.example.appname\n\nspecifying the app bundle identifier as appropriate.\n\nBriefcase *can* be published to Flathub or another Flatpak repository; but\nBriefcase does not currently support automated publication of apps.\n\nIcon format\n===========\n\nFlatpak uses ``.png`` format icons. An application must provide icons in\nthe following sizes:\n\n* 16px\n* 32px\n* 64px\n* 128px\n* 256px\n* 512px\n\nFlatpaks do not support splash screens or installer images.\n\nApplication configuration\n=========================\n\nThe following options can be added to the\n``tool.briefcase.app.<appname>.linux.flatpak`` section of your\n``pyproject.toml`` file:\n\n``finish_arg``\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n\nThe arguments used to configure the Flatpak sandbox. ``finish_arg`` is an attribute\nthat can have additional sub-attributes; each sub-attribute maps to a single property\nthat will be added to the app's manifest. For example, to add ``--allow=bluetooth`` as a\nfinish argument, you would specify::\n\n    finish_arg.\"allow=bluetooth\" = True\n\nBriefcase adds the following finish arguments by default:\n\n* ``share=ipc``\n* ``socket=x11``\n* ``nosocket=wayland``\n* ``share=network``\n* ``device=dri``\n* ``socket=pulseaudio``\n* ``filesystem=xdg-cache``\n* ``filesystem=xdg-config``\n* ``filesystem=xdg-data``\n* ``filesystem=xdg-documents``\n* ``socket=session-bus``\n\nThese can be disabled by explicitly setting their value to ``False``; for example, to\ndisable audio access, you would specify::\n\n    finish_arg.\"socket=pulseaudio\" = false\n\n``flatpak_runtime_repo_alias``\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n\nAn alias to use when registering the Flatpak repository that will store the\nFlatpak runtime used to build the app. By default, Briefcase will use `Flathub\n<https://flathub.org>`__ as its runtime repository, with an alias of ``flathub``.\n\n``flatpak_runtime_repo_url``\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n\nThe repository URL hosting the runtime and SDK package that the Flatpak will\nuse. By default, Briefcase will use `Flathub <https://flathub.org>`__ as its\nruntime repository.\n\n``flatpak_runtime``\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n\nA string, identifying the `runtime\n<https://docs.flatpak.org/en/latest/available-runtimes.html>`__ to use as a\nbase for the Flatpak app.\n\nThe Flatpak runtime and SDK are paired; so, both a ``flatpak_runtime`` and a\ncorresponding ``flatpak_sdk`` must be defined.\n\n``flatpak_runtime_version``\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n\nA string, identifying the version of the Flatpak runtime that should be used.\n\n``flatpak_sdk``\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n\nA string, identifying the SDK associated with the platform that will be used to\nbuild the Flatpak app.\n\nThe Flatpak runtime and SDK are paired; so, both a ``flatpak_runtime`` and a\ncorresponding ``flatpak_sdk`` must be defined.\n\n``modules_extra_content``\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n\nAdditional build instructions that will be inserted into the Flatpak manifest, *after*\nPython has been installed and ``pip`` is guaranteed to exist, but *before* any app code\nor app packages have been installed into the Flatpak.\n\nPermissions\n===========\n\nPermissions are not used for Flatpak packaging.\n\nCompilation issues with Flatpak\n===============================\n\nFlatpak works by building a sandbox in which to compile the application bundle.\nThis sandbox uses some low-level kernel and file system operations to provide\nthe sandboxing behavior. As a result, Flatpaks cannot be built inside a Docker\ncontainer, and they cannot be build on an NFS mounted drive.\n\nIf you get errors about ``renameat`` when building an app, similar to the\nfollowing::\n\n    [helloworld] Building Flatpak...\n    Downloading sources\n    Initializing build dir\n    Committing stage init to cache\n    Error: Writing metadata object: renameat: Operation not permitted\n    Building...\n\n    Error while building app helloworld.\n\n    Log saved to ...\n\nyou may be building on an NFS drive. Move your project to local storage, and\nretry the build.\n","numLines":180,"startLine":1,"totalLines":180}}},{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-D1ZhGdn5l6s8KBYOg9MGDAXCz2b8K","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_IcMtvtsm2MZ6nNgpRc2MNS1q","name":"Grep","input":{"pattern":"finish_args","path":"/testbed","output_mode":"files_with_matches"}}],"model":"gpt-5-2025-08-07","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":18626,"output_tokens":422},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"010a84c9-f0d8-4259-8033-5fc61c44e166","uuid":"5c5f6c43-6eab-439a-b15a-1fce9f1d0d92"},{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_IcMtvtsm2MZ6nNgpRc2MNS1q","type":"tool_result","content":"Found 2 files\nsrc/briefcase/platforms/linux/flatpak.py\ntests/platforms/linux/flatpak/test_create.py"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"010a84c9-f0d8-4259-8033-5fc61c44e166","uuid":"153794af-930d-4e15-8e97-e8a71a965223","tool_use_result":{"mode":"files_with_matches","filenames":["src/briefcase/platforms/linux/flatpak.py","tests/platforms/linux/flatpak/test_create.py"],"numFiles":2}},{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-D1ZhcQ7JF7Si3c1UnXE6Ti12lJFKK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_AO60Xy33CuDU0OZeMeOkVanL","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/testbed/src/briefcase/integrations/flatpak.py"}}],"model":"gpt-5-2025-08-07","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":18695,"output_tokens":485},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"010a84c9-f0d8-4259-8033-5fc61c44e166","uuid":"98be741c-6cc2-413e-ba9d-9803c5172352"},{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_AO60Xy33CuDU0OZeMeOkVanL","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→from __future__ import annotations\n     2→\n     3→import subprocess\n     4→from pathlib import Path\n     5→\n     6→from briefcase.exceptions import BriefcaseCommandError\n     7→from briefcase.integrations.base import Tool, ToolCache\n     8→from briefcase.integrations.subprocess import SubprocessArgT\n     9→\n    10→\n    11→class Flatpak(Tool):\n    12→    name = \"flatpak\"\n    13→    full_name = \"Flatpak\"\n    14→    supported_host_os = {\"Linux\"}\n    15→\n    16→    DEFAULT_REPO_ALIAS = \"flathub\"\n    17→    DEFAULT_REPO_URL = \"https://flathub.org/repo/flathub.flatpakrepo\"\n    18→\n    19→    @classmethod\n    20→    def verify_install(cls, tools: ToolCache, **kwargs) -> Flatpak:\n    21→        \"\"\"Verify that the Flatpak toolchain is available.\n    22→\n    23→        :param tools: ToolCache of available tools\n    24→        :returns: A wrapper for the Flatpak tools.\n    25→        \"\"\"\n    26→        # short circuit since already verified and available\n    27→        if hasattr(tools, \"flatpak\"):\n    28→            return tools.flatpak\n    29→\n    30→        flatpak = Flatpak(tools=tools)\n    31→        try:\n    32→            output = tools.subprocess.check_output([\"flatpak\", \"--version\"]).strip(\"\\n\")\n    33→            parts = output.split(\" \")\n    34→            try:\n    35→                if parts[0] == \"Flatpak\":\n    36→                    version = parts[1].split(\".\")\n    37→                    if int(version[0]) < 1:\n    38→                        raise BriefcaseCommandError(\n    39→                            \"Briefcase requires Flatpak 1.0 or later.\"\n    40→                        )\n    41→                else:\n    42→                    raise ValueError(f\"Unexpected tool name {parts[0]}\")\n    43→            except (ValueError, IndexError):\n    44→                tools.logger.warning(\n    45→                    \"\"\"\\\n    46→*************************************************************************\n    47→** WARNING: Unable to determine the version of Flatpak                 **\n    48→*************************************************************************\n    49→\n    50→    Briefcase will proceed, assuming everything is OK. If you\n    51→    experience problems, this is almost certainly the cause of those\n    52→    problems.\n    53→\n    54→    Please report this as a bug at:\n    55→\n    56→      https://github.com/beeware/briefcase/issues/new\n    57→\n    58→    In your report, please including the output from running:\n    59→\n    60→      flatpak --version\n    61→\n    62→    from the command prompt.\n    63→\n    64→*************************************************************************\n    65→\"\"\"\n    66→                )\n    67→\n    68→        except OSError as e:\n    69→            raise BriefcaseCommandError(\n    70→                \"\"\"\\\n    71→Briefcase requires the Flatpak toolchain, but it does not appear to be installed.\n    72→\n    73→Instructions for installing the Flatpak toolchain can be found at:\n    74→\n    75→    https://flatpak.org/setup/\n    76→\n    77→You must install both flatpak and flatpak-builder.\n    78→\"\"\"\n    79→            ) from e\n    80→        except subprocess.CalledProcessError as e:\n    81→            raise BriefcaseCommandError(\"Unable to invoke flatpak.\") from e\n    82→\n    83→        try:\n    84→            output = tools.subprocess.check_output(\n    85→                [\"flatpak-builder\", \"--version\"]\n    86→            ).strip(\"\\n\")\n    87→\n    88→            # flatpak-builder 1.3 changed the output of --version\n    89→            # from \"flatpak-builder 1.2.X\" to \"flatpak-build-1.3.X\".\n    90→            # Converge on the new-style format.\n    91→            parts = output.replace(\" \", \"-\").rsplit(\"-\", 1)\n    92→            try:\n    93→                if parts[0] == \"flatpak-builder\":\n    94→                    version = parts[1].split(\".\")\n    95→                    if int(version[0]) < 1:\n    96→                        raise BriefcaseCommandError(\n    97→                            \"Briefcase requires flatpak-builder 1.0 or later.\"\n    98→                        )\n    99→                else:\n   100→                    raise ValueError(f\"Unexpected tool name {parts[0]}\")\n   101→            except (ValueError, IndexError):\n   102→                tools.logger.warning(\n   103→                    \"\"\"\\\n   104→*************************************************************************\n   105→** WARNING: Unable to determine the version of flatpak-builder         **\n   106→*************************************************************************\n   107→\n   108→    Briefcase will proceed, assuming everything is OK. If you\n   109→    experience problems, this is almost certainly the cause of those\n   110→    problems.\n   111→\n   112→    Please report this as a bug at:\n   113→\n   114→      https://github.com/beeware/briefcase/issues/new\n   115→\n   116→    In your report, please including the output from running:\n   117→\n   118→      flatpak-builder --version\n   119→\n   120→    from the command prompt.\n   121→\n   122→*************************************************************************\n   123→\"\"\"\n   124→                )\n   125→\n   126→        except OSError as e:\n   127→            raise BriefcaseCommandError(\n   128→                \"\"\"\\\n   129→Briefcase requires the full Flatpak development toolchain, but flatpak-builder\n   130→does not appear to be installed.\n   131→\n   132→Instructions for installing the Flatpak toolchain can be found at:\n   133→\n   134→    https://flatpak.org/setup/\n   135→\n   136→You must install both flatpak and flatpak-builder.\n   137→\"\"\"\n   138→            ) from e\n   139→        except subprocess.CalledProcessError as e:\n   140→            raise BriefcaseCommandError(\"Unable to invoke flatpak-builder.\") from